  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "The structure of calcium carbide is:<br /><br /><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Ca</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mo>&#8801;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></mrow></mfenced><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>-</mo></mrow></msup></math><br /><br />Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi></math> atoms are&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>sp</mi></math> hybridised. The carbide anion, having <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn></math> units of negative charge between two carbon atoms, has&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#963;</mi></math>&nbsp;and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi></math>&nbsp;bonds.<br />&nbsp;",
    "image": null
  },
